*=================HELP FOR CREATE NEW PDS TEMPLATE PROGRAM ================
*TITLE
Title   : Identifies the title of the Design. Use any combination
          of up to 59 alphanumeric characters in the title.  The title
          can include the characters a-z, the numerals 0-9, the period,
          and the underscore character.  The software generates a
          warning if the Title statement is omitted, but continues
          processing.
*PATTERN
Pattern : Provides a place to document the pattern (if any) or other
          identifying code for the file. The pattern name can include
          the characters a-z, the numerals 0-9, the period,
          and the underscore character.  The software generates a
          warning if the Pattern statement is omitted, but continues
          processing.
*REVISION
Revision: Identifies the revision (if any) of a MACHXL design file. The
          revision name can include the characters a-z, the numerals 0-9,
          the period, and the underscore character.  The software generates
          a warning if the Revision statement is omitted, but continues
          processing.
*AUTHOR
Author  : Identifies the author(s) of the MACHXL design. The author name(s)
          can include the characters a-z, the numerals 0-9, the period,
          and the underscore character.  The software generates a warning
          if the Author statement is omitted, but continues processing.
*COMPANY
Company : Identifies the Company or other Business id of the creator of
          the MACHXL design file. The company name can include the
          characters a-z, the numerals 0-9, the period, and the underscore
          character.  The software generates a warning if the Company
          statement is omitted, but continues processing.
*DATE
The system automatically enters today's date which it gets from your
computer system.  You can overtype this with another date if you prefer.

Date    : Identifies the Date this design was created. The date can include
          the characters a-z, the numerals 0-9, the period, and the
          underscore character.  The software generates a warning if the
          Date statement is omitted, but continues processing.
*CHIPNAME
The Chip Name assigns a name to the chip. Up to 14 alphanumeric characters
may be used. The CHIP statement is automatically constructed by the program
using the design name but may be overwritten by a name of your choice.

Characters allowed are:-   'A - Z', 'a - z', '0 - 9', '_' 
 
                          This entry is REQUIRED!
*DEVICENAME 
The device to be used for this design must be selected from the list that
is provided.
              eg. MACH110
                  MACH120
                  etc.

Highlight the desired device and press <enter>
*PINNODE
Select PIN to begin a PIN statement.  Select NODE to begin a NODE
statement.
*PINNUMBER
PIN NUMBER can be from 1 to Maximum number of physical pins on the
selected device or may be specified by '?' which allows the software
to determine the best placement for the pin.

NODE NUMBER can be from 1 to Maximum number of nodes defined for the
selected device or may be specified by '?' which allows the software
to determine the best placement for the node.

Input Format is either as <number> or <range> where:-

	<number>  = integer 1 to max Pin/Node number
	<range>   = <number>..<number>
		  = <number>,<number>
		  	
Check the manual for a more complete description of the syntax.
*PINNAME
PIN/NODE name is a logical name assigned to the PIN/NODE. All design
signals must be described in terms of the PIN/NODE name. The following
rules apply:

Each name must be unique.

Input Format is either as <name> or name[<range>] where:-
        <name>    = identifier using the characters: -
			'A - Z', 'a - z', '0 - 9', '_' 
		    The first character must be Alphabetic.

	<number>  = integer
	<range>   = <number>..<number>
		  = <number>,<number>
		  	
check manual for exact syntax.
*PAIRED
OUTPUT PAIRING - use with a NODE definition:

Use output pairing to associate a node with an output pin.  To perform
explicit output pairing specify the name of a pin to which the declared
node should be associated.  The software will add the keyword PAIR to your
node statement, and will append the name of the pin specified in this field.


INPUT PAIRING - use with a PIN definition:

Use input pairing to associate a pin with a node.  To perform explicit
input pairing specify the name of a node to which the declared pin should
be associated.  The software will add the keyword PAIR to your pin statement
and will append the name of the node specified in this field.

For more information on pairing please refer to the MACHXL manual.

*STORAGE

Select COMBINATIORIAL, REGISTERED, LATCHED or BLANK for the pin/node being
defined.  Blank defaults to combinatorial, but allows a pin or node to
take its storage-type definition from the node or pin with which it is
paired.  See the manual or a data sheet for information about which
storage types are valid for each device.
*COMMENT

Select one of the available comments or leave the field blank.  Comments
have no effect on design processing.  Any text that follows a semicolon is
treated as a comment and is ignored by MACHXL.  All comments are for user
information only.

